    content: "Adventists understand God's law as a transcript of His character — eternal, unchanging, and inseparable from who He is. The Ten Commandments are not arbitrary rules imposed by divine authority; they describe the way reality works when love governs all relationships. The first four commandments address love toward God; the last six address love toward neighbor. Together they constitute a complete ethical framework rooted in the two great principles Jesus identified in Matthew 22:37-40.\n\n**Covenant context, not cold legalism.** Adventist theology insists that the law must always be read within the covenant relationship. Exodus 20:2 is not a throwaway introduction; it is the interpretive key. God acts first in grace, and the commandments describe the grateful response of a redeemed people. Any presentation of the law that divorces it from grace distorts the biblical picture. When obedience becomes a means of earning salvation rather than a response to salvation already received, the result is legalism — a distortion Adventists explicitly reject.\n\n**Christ and the law.** Adventists affirm that Jesus perfectly embodied the law in His life, demonstrating that obedience is possible through dependence on the Father and the indwelling Spirit. His fulfillment of the law does not cancel it but reveals its true depth. The Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5-7) is not a replacement of the Decalogue but its authoritative interpretation. Anger, lust, and broken promises are exposed as violations of the commandments' intent, not merely their letter.\n\n**The Great Controversy dimension.** Within the Adventist framework, God's law is central to the cosmic conflict between good and evil. Satan's original accusation was that God's law is arbitrary and impossible to keep. Christ's perfect life answered that charge. The redeemed community — empowered by the Spirit — continues to answer it by demonstrating that love-based obedience is both possible and joyful. Revelation 12:17 and 14:12 describe God's end-time people as those who \"keep the commandments of God and have the faith of Jesus\" — a dual identity that holds law and grace in permanent union.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against antinomianism — the belief that grace eliminates the need for obedience. It equally guards against legalism — the belief that obedience earns grace. The biblical balance is grace-motivated, Spirit-empowered, love-expressed obedience.",
