    title: "SDA Theological Integration",
    content: "Adventist Sabbath theology rests on three interconnected pillars: creation, redemption, and sanctification. Each pillar deepens the meaning of the seventh day beyond mere day-of-rest observance.\n\n**Creation memorial.** As established in Lesson 1, the Sabbath is rooted in Genesis 2:1-3 — a pre-fall, pre-national institution given to all humanity. Adventists observe the seventh day (Saturday) because the commandment specifies \"the seventh day\" and grounds it in God's creative act. The particular day matters because the blessing and sanctification were placed on a specific day, not on a generic concept of rest.\n\n**Redemption sign.** Deuteronomy 5:15 adds the exodus dimension: the Sabbath celebrates liberation from bondage. Adventists see in this a profound spiritual application — just as Israel was freed from Egyptian slavery, believers are freed from the slavery of sin. Sabbath rest becomes a weekly declaration of freedom: \"I rest because God has already done the work of delivering me.\" This connects directly to the gospel: the believer rests not in personal achievement but in Christ's finished work.\n\n**Sanctification sign.** Ezekiel 20:12, 20 identifies the Sabbath as the sign by which God's people know He is the one who sanctifies them. Adventists understand this as the Sabbath's relational function — it is the covenant marker that distinguishes God's people, not by ethnic identity or external ritual, but by their trust in the God who sets apart and transforms. The Sabbath answers the question \"Whose are you?\" by pointing to the Creator-Redeemer who sanctifies.\n\n**Jesus as Lord of the Sabbath.** Mark 2:27-28 ensures that Sabbath-keeping never degenerates into legalistic rigidity. Jesus, as Lord of the Sabbath, defines its observance in terms of human wholeness — healing, nourishment, compassion, and worship. Adventists reject both the Pharisaic excess of multiplied regulations and the modern dismissal of the day as irrelevant. The Sabbath is a gift to be received with joy, not a burden to be endured with reluctance.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against reducing the Sabbath to either a merely cultural practice or a test of spiritual superiority. The Sabbath is received, not achieved. It is a sign of trust, not a badge of merit.",
